\begin_body

\begin_layout Section
Generalization bounds:
 Finite and Countably Infinity Hypothesis Classes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Single hypothesis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Setting and risk definitions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume the loss function 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell(h(X),Y)$
\end_inset

 is bounded on 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 This is important for the specific calculations but the general insights are applicable to any loss function bounds 
\begin_inset Formula $[a,b]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
True risk
\series default
:

\series bold
 
\series default
The quality of any hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is measured by its (true) risk
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L(h)\equiv\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim p}\left[\ell(h(X),Y)\right],
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is the 
\emph on
unknown
\emph default
 distribution over which the world has generated 
\begin_inset Formula $(X,Y)$
\end_inset

 - i.e.
 pairs of features from the input space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

 and outcomes from the label space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{Y}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Empirical risk
\series default
:
 Using a validation sample
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S=\left\{ (X_{1},Y_{1}),\dots,(X_{N},Y_{N})\right\} ,\quad(X_{i},Y_{i})\overset{iid}{\sim}p,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
we calculate the empirical risk defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{L}(h,S)\equiv\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Theoretical properties of empirical risk
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Unbiased:
 
\series default
Since the validation samples are independently and identically distributed from the distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[\hat{L}(h,S)\right] & =\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n})\right]\\
 & =\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\mathbb{E}_{(X_{n},Y_{n})\sim p}\left[\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n})\right]\\
 & =\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\mathbb{E}_{(X_{1},Y_{1})\sim p}\left[\ell(h(X_{1}),Y_{1})\right]\\
 & =\mathbb{E}_{(X_{1},Y_{1})\sim p}\left[\ell(h(X_{1}),Y_{1})\right]\\
 & =L(h).
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However the above only holds because 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 If not we generally have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[\hat{L}\left(\hat{h}_{S},S\right)\right]\neq\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[L\left(\hat{h}_{S}\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
What can be said about the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{L}(h,S)$
\end_inset

?
 
\series default
Formally what is 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 here
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)-\hat{L}(h,S)\geq\epsilon\right)\leq\delta.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I.e.
 with what tightness can we bound the probability that the true risk 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

 bigger than 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{L}$
\end_inset

.
 For example we might want a confindence of 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta=0.05$
\end_inset

 that is w.p.
 less than 
\begin_inset Formula $0.05$
\end_inset

 the true loss 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=f(\delta)$
\end_inset

 - that depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 units bigger than 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Applying Hoeffdings bound yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)-\hat{L}(h,S)\geq\epsilon\right) & =\mathbb{P}\left(\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n})\right]}_{=\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(h(X_{1}),Y_{1})\right]=L(h)}-\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n})\geq\epsilon\right)\\
 & =\mathbb{P}\left(\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n})\right]-\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n})\geq N\epsilon\right)\\
 & \leq\exp\left\{ -\frac{2(N\epsilon)^{2}}{\sum^{N}_{n=1}(b_{n}-a_{n})^{2}}\right\} \\
 & =\exp\left\{ -2N\epsilon^{2}\right\} .
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Defining the relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta=\exp\left\{ -2N\epsilon^{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

 yields 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)\geq\hat{L}(h,S)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}\right) & \leq\delta,
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and taking the complement
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)<\hat{L}(h,S)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}\right)=1-\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)\geq\hat{L}(h,S)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}\right)\geq1-\delta.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Sidenote:
 Understanding 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Earlier we obtained
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)-\hat{L}(h,S)\geq\epsilon\right)\leq\exp\left\{ -2N\epsilon^{2}\right\} .
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
we can think if this inequality as asking:
 
\emph on
If you set an error-margin 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

 then I tell you how confident I am that an error happens (i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $L-\hat{L}\geq\epsilon$
\end_inset

).
 
\emph default
If we instead ask the question:
 
\emph on
I want the probability that an error happens 
\begin_inset Formula $(i.e.L-\hat{L}\geq\epsilon)$
\end_inset

 to be 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta\cdot100\%$
\end_inset

 how large does the error-margin 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

 have to be?

\emph default
 The latter case is answered by relabeling the right hand side as 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta=\exp\left\{ -2N\epsilon^{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

 and then backing out what 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

 must be:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The complement flips the question:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)<\hat{L}(h,S)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}\right)\geq1-\delta,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and asks:
 The probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 is acceptable (i.e.
 smaller than 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{L}(h,S)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}$
\end_inset

) is at least 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\delta$
\end_inset

.
 Or:
 I am 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\delta)\cdot100\%$
\end_inset

 certain that 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 is smaller than 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{L}(h,S)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Finite Hypothesis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike before we have now trained 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{1},\dots,h_{M}$
\end_inset

 hypotheses and chosen 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{h^{*}_{S}}$
\end_inset

 with empirical risk minimization 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\hat{h^{*}_{S}} & =\arg\min_{h\in\mathcal{H}}\hat{L}(h,S)\\
 & =\arg\min_{h\in\mathcal{H}}\left\{ \frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n})\right\} ,
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where
\series bold
 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset


\series default
 is a validation sample (i.e.
 not used for training) with elements 
\begin_inset Formula $(X_{n},Y_{n})\overset{iid}{\sim}p$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Hoeffdings bound does not apply anymore!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The question is as follows:
 
\emph on
How bad can we expect our model to perform?

\emph default
 This is measured in terms of (true) risk
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim p}\left[\ell\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}(X),Y\right)\right],
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is the probability distribution of the stochastic vector 
\begin_inset Formula $(X,Y)$
\end_inset

.
 We would ideally just plug and play with Hoeffding's inequality.
 However this requires that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}\right)=\mathbb{E}_{S}\left[\hat{L}\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}},S\right)\right],
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
since
\family typewriter

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}\left(L\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}\right)-\hat{L}\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}},S\right)\geq\epsilon\right) & =\mathbb{P}\left(L\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}\right)-\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}(X_{n}),Y_{n}\right)\geq\epsilon\right)\\
 & =\mathbb{P}\left(NL\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}\right)-\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}(X_{n}),Y_{n}\right)\geq N\epsilon\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For Hoeffdings bound to apply we would therefore need that
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
To see this simply look at the inequality
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{N}_{n=1}X_{n}\right]-\sum^{N}_{n=1}X_{n}\geq\epsilon\right)\leq\exp\left\{ -\frac{2\epsilon^{2}}{\sum^{N}_{n=1}(b_{n}-a_{n})^{2}}\right\} ,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
from where we see that the left term needs to be 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{N}_{n=1}X_{n}\right]$
\end_inset

 i.e.
 the expectation of the right term 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum^{N}_{n=1}X_{n}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
NL\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}\right)=\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}(X_{n}),Y_{n}\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
which implies 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}\right)=\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}(X_{n}),Y_{n}\right)\right]=\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[\hat{L}\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}},S\right)\right].
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This however does not generally hold since
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[\hat{L}\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}},S\right)\right] & =\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}(X_{n}),Y_{n}\right)\right]\\
 & \neq\mathbb{E}_{(X_{1},Y_{1})\sim p}\left[\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}(X_{1}),Y_{1}\right)\right],
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{h^{*}}_{S}$
\end_inset

 generally depends on the whole sample 
\begin_inset Formula $S=\{(X_{1},Y_{1}),\dots,(X_{N},Y_{N})\}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore the stochastic variables 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell\left(\hat{h^{*}}_{S}\left(X_{n}\right),Y_{n}\right)$
\end_inset

 are not independent (because 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{h^{*}}_{S}$
\end_inset

 was selected using 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{-(X_{n},Y_{n})}$
\end_inset

) and therefore Hoeffding's inequality does not apply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
The solution
\series default
:
 Therefore we somehow need to break dependence of the hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{h^{*}}_{S}$
\end_inset

 on the sample:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}\left(L\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}\right)-\hat{L}\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}},S\right)\geq\epsilon\right) & \leq\mathbb{P}\left(L\left(h_{1}\right)-\hat{L}\left(h_{1},S\right)\geq\epsilon\text{ or }\cdots\text{ or }L\left(h_{M}\right)-\hat{L}\left(h_{M},S\right)\geq\epsilon\right)\\
 & =\mathbb{P}\left(\exists h\in\mathcal{H}:L\left(h\right)-\hat{L}\left(h,S\right)\geq\epsilon\right)\\
 & \leq\sum^{M}_{j=1}\mathbb{P}\left(L\left(h_{j}\right)-\hat{L}\left(h_{j},S\right)\geq\epsilon\right)\\
 & =\sum^{M}_{j=1}\mathbb{P}\left(N\underbrace{L\left(h_{j}\right)}_{\equiv\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim p}\left[\ell\left(h_{j}(X),Y\right)\right]}-\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell\left(h_{j}(X_{n}),Y_{n}\right)\geq N\epsilon\right)\\
 & =\sum^{M}_{j=1}\mathbb{P}\left(N\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell\left(h_{j}(X),Y\right)\right]-\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell\left(h_{j}(X_{n}),Y_{n}\right)\geq N\epsilon\right)\\
 & \leq M\exp\left\{ -\frac{2(N\epsilon)^{2}}{\sum^{N}_{n=1}(b_{n}-a_{n})^{2}}\right\} \\
 & =M\exp\left\{ -2N\epsilon^{2}\right\} ,
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first inequality holds simply because 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{h^{*}_{S}}\in\left\{ h_{1},h_{2},\dots,h_{M}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 The second inequality holds by the union bound:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(A\cup B\right)=P(A)+P(B)-\mathbb{P}\left(A\cap B\right)\leq P(A)+P(B).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From this we obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\delta=M\exp\left\{ -2N\epsilon^{2}\right\} \iff\epsilon=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{M}{\delta}\right)},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and further
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}\left(L\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}\right)\geq\hat{L}\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}},S\right)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{M}{\delta}\right)}\right) & \leq\delta\\
\mathbb{P}\left(L\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}\right)<\hat{L}\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}},S\right)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{M}{\delta}\right)}\right) & \geq1-\delta.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
This is the result
\series default
:
 From a finite predetermined hypothesis class with cardinality 
\begin_inset Formula $|\mathcal{H}|=M$
\end_inset

 using the Empirical Risk Minimizer (ERM) - or any selection over 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

!
 Since:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(\exists h\in\mathcal{H}:L\left(h\right)\geq\hat{L}\left(h,S\right)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{M}{\delta}\right)}\right)\leq\delta,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and in particular for the ERM hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{h^{*}_{S}}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(L\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}}\right)\geq\hat{L}\left(\hat{h^{*}_{S}},S\right)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{M}{\delta}\right)}\right)\leq\delta.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Lower Bound
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In general there is a lower bound on how well bounding inequalities can work.
 Concretely we use Hoeffding's inequality,
 but could this be improved?
 And how much?
 To answer the first:
 yes,
 it has been improved.
 For the second question:
 we can show that there is a limit to how much bounding can happen in general.
 We call this a lower bound which answers the question:
 In general what is the lowest upper bound we can impose on an event?
 or:
 It tells us that we can never be 
\emph on
this
\emph default
 certain (i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 cannot be this low) using bounding techniques.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In short the lectures construct an example for a specific hypothesis space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(\exists h\in\mathcal{H}:L\left(h\right)\geq\hat{L}\left(h,S\right)+\frac{1}{8}\right)\geq\frac{1}{8}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Approximation and Estimation error
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Approximation error comes from restricting 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 so that the Bayes optimal hypothestical choice is omittet (i.e.
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 that would generate the lowest 
\begin_inset Formula $L(h)$
\end_inset

 is not in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Estimation defines the variance in selecting the best hypothesis in class 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

:
 there is an 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{*}=\arg\min_{h\in\mathcal{H}}L(h)$
\end_inset

 but using data we are not sure to find it.
 we most likely find 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\hat{h})<L(h^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 This is the estimation error.
\end_layout

